#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# find_linear_recurrence

#% text
# `find_linear_recurrence(xs)` gives the coefficients [c1, ..., cL] of the shortest linear recurrence x_n = c1 x_(n-1) + ... + cL x_(n-L) with constant coefficients that the list xs of exact numbers or expressions satisfies; `find_linear_recurrence(xs, d)` looks only for orders up to d.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The recurrence comes from the algorithm of Berlekamp and Massey (Massey, IEEE Transactions on Information Theory 15, 1969). It counts as found only when the list checks it beyond the 2 L terms that determine it; otherwise the result is a failure.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
find_linear_recurrence(table(2^k, [k, 10]))
#% output
#= [2]

#% cell
find_linear_recurrence(table(fibonacci(k), [k, 10]))
#% output
#= [1, 1]

#% cell
find_linear_recurrence(table(fibonacci(k)*fibonacci(k - 1)^2, [k, 12]))
#% output
#= [3, 6, -3, -1]

#% text
# The recurrence reproduces the list with `linear_recurrence`:

#% cell
{ let xs = table(k^3 + 2^k, [k, 12]); let c = find_linear_recurrence(xs); linear_recurrence(c, take(xs, length(c)), 12) == xs }
#% output
#= true

#% text
# Entries in symbols:

#% cell
find_linear_recurrence(table(a^k + k*b^k, [k, 10]))
#% output
#= [a + 2*b, -b^2 - 2*a*b, a*b^2]

#% text
# A list too short to check a recurrence:

#% cell
find_linear_recurrence([1, 2, 4, 9])
#% output
#! failure(no_recurrence)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `linear_recurrence`, `find_generating_function`, `find_sequence_function`
